---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc850.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Who Is a Related Party?" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Related Party Definition — Common Categories" },
        { id: "s4", label: "No Special Measurement — Disclosure Only" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Required Disclosures" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The Key Divergence — IAS 24's Key Management Personnel Compensation Categorisation" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Related Party Disclosure" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements (Detailed)" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "ASC 850 vs IAS 24 — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Transparency about who an entity really transacts with</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Transactions between an entity and its RELATED PARTIES (management, significant owners, affiliated entities) may not always occur on genuinely ARM'S-LENGTH terms — a related party transaction might be priced, timed, or structured differently than it would be between unrelated parties. ASC 850 doesn't change HOW such transactions are measured or recognised (that's determined by whatever OTHER Topic governs the transaction type) — it simply requires DISCLOSURE, so financial statement users can identify and evaluate these relationships and transactions for themselves.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Who Is a Related Party?</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Control, significant influence, or common ownership</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 850 applies to ALL entities preparing financial statements under US GAAP, requiring identification of RELATED PARTIES — broadly, parties that can CONTROL or SIGNIFICANTLY INFLUENCE the reporting entity's management or operating policies, or vice versa, such that they might not act in their own separate best interests.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Related Party Definition — Common Categories</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Who typically qualifies</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Category</span><span>Description</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Affiliates</span><span>Entities that control, are controlled by, or are under common control with the reporting entity</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Equity method investees</span><span>Entities in which the reporting entity has significant influence</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Principal owners</span><span>Owners of more than 10% of the entity's voting interests</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Management</span><span>Persons responsible for achieving the entity's objectives, including their immediate family members</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 2fr;"><span>Other parties</span><span>Any party able to significantly influence management or operating policies such that an arm's-length transaction might not result</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>No Special Measurement — Disclosure Only</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">The transaction is still measured under its own Topic's rules</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 850 does NOT itself REQUIRE (or preclude) any special RECOGNITION or MEASUREMENT treatment for related party transactions — a related party sale is still measured under ASC 606, a related party loan still measured under the applicable financial instruments guidance, and so on. ASC 850's role is purely to require adequate DISCLOSURE of the relationship and the transaction, so readers can judge for themselves whether the terms appear arm's-length.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Required Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Nature, amounts, and balances</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">For MATERIAL related party transactions, ASC 850 requires disclosure of: the NATURE of the relationship; a DESCRIPTION of the transactions, including transactions with no amounts ascribed to them; the DOLLAR AMOUNT of transactions for each period presented; and AMOUNTS DUE TO/FROM related parties as of each balance sheet date, including terms and manner of settlement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Key Divergence — IAS 24's Key Management Personnel Compensation Categorisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A more granular, mandatory compensation breakdown that ASC 850 doesn't require</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;"><strong>IAS 24 REQUIRES</strong> disclosure of KEY MANAGEMENT PERSONNEL (KMP) compensation broken down into SPECIFIC, MANDATORY CATEGORIES: short-term employee benefits, post-employment benefits, other long-term benefits, termination benefits, and share-based payment — a genuinely PRESCRIPTIVE categorisation structure. <strong>ASC 850 is LESS PRESCRIPTIVE</strong> — it does NOT mandate this SAME granular breakdown of management/executive compensation by category within its related party disclosures (though executive compensation disclosure requirements DO exist elsewhere, notably in SEC proxy disclosure rules for public companies, which are a SEPARATE regulatory requirement, not an ASC 850 requirement itself). This is a genuine, worth-noting divergence in disclosure GRANULARITY and STRUCTURE between the two frameworks' related party standards specifically.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Related Party Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Disclosing a related party lease</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An entity leasing warehouse space from its CEO</div><div class="wk-body">An entity leases warehouse space from a company wholly owned by its CEO, paying $240,000 in annual rent — comparable to, but not independently benchmarked against, market rates in the area.</div><div class="wk-answer">The entity DISCLOSES: the NATURE of the relationship (lessor is wholly owned by the entity's CEO); a DESCRIPTION of the arrangement (warehouse lease); the DOLLAR AMOUNT of rent paid each period ($240,000 annually); and any amounts DUE TO/FROM the related lessor as of the balance sheet date (e.g. any unpaid rent or security deposit balance). The lease itself continues to be MEASURED under ASC 842's normal lessee accounting rules — ASC 850 doesn't change that measurement, only requires the disclosure.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Detailed)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 850 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of the relationship(s) involved</span><span>Description of transactions, including those with no dollar amounts</span><span>Dollar amount of transactions for each period an income statement is presented</span><span>Amounts due to/from related parties, terms, and manner of settlement</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying all related parties, particularly less obvious "other parties" able to exert significant influence</span><span>Assessing materiality of related party transactions for disclosure purposes</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — this Topic is disclosure-focused rather than measurement-focused</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>ASC 850 vs IAS 24 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 850 vs IAS 24</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 850)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 24)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Special measurement/recognition</span><span>NONE — disclosure only</span><span>NONE — disclosure only, converged approach</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Basic related party definition</span><span>Control, significant influence, principal owners, management</span><span>Broadly similar — control, joint control, significant influence, KMP</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>KMP compensation disclosure</span><span>NOT separately mandated by category within ASC 850 itself</span><span>REQUIRED, broken into specific mandatory categories (short-term, post-employment, other long-term, termination, share-based payment)</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Government-related entity exemption</span><span>No equivalent broad exemption</span><span>IAS 24 provides a partial exemption for entities related solely through government control</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The KMP compensation categorisation gap is the most consequential remaining divergence — IAS 24's approach gives readers a genuinely more granular, standardised breakdown of executive compensation than ASC 850 mandates on its own.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where ASC 850 disclosures land</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Related party transaction disclosure</span><span>Notes only</span><span>The underlying transaction itself is recognised/measured under its own applicable Topic</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 810</span><span>Consolidation — supplies the control concepts underlying related party identification for affiliates</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 323</span><span>Equity method investees are a common category of related party</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 718</span><span>Governs measurement of share-based payments, a component of KMP compensation under IAS 24's more granular disclosure</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Related party</div><div class="glossary-def">A party able to control or significantly influence the reporting entity's management or operating policies, or vice versa.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Principal owner</div><div class="glossary-def">An owner of more than 10% of an entity's voting interests, a specifically named related party category under ASC 850.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Key management personnel (KMP)</div><div class="glossary-def">Individuals with authority and responsibility for planning, directing, and controlling an entity's activities — the subject of IAS 24's mandatory compensation categorisation, not separately required in the same structured way under ASC 850.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC850 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

